"""Local checks for the 2026-11-26 post-cost cut (the owner's
cost-cutting pass: "make that and Moonfactory way cheaper",
then clarified "cut OUR cost, not our users' prices").

What is pinned:

- The true post cost is the re-measured figure: the finishing
  chain for an API-lane job (download, loudnorm/mux with the
  video stream copied, SHA-256 stamp, output classifier frame
  grabs and ONNX inferences, probes) totals roughly 2 to 3.5
  CPU core-seconds per job, about 0.0006c per finished second
  at Modal's published CPU price; POST_COST_CENTS_PER_SECOND
  is 0.01, a 10x cut from the 0.1 pad carried since 2026-11-16
  and still several times the padded worst case.
- The owner pays the cut figure: every per-second lane's owner
  sell rate is provider rate + 0.01 (the FrameHush Seedance
  worked example: 3.51 / 4.71 / 8.01 cents per second), and a
  flat lane's true raw carries 0.01 per finished second.
- NO customer price moves. Customer sell rates, minimums and
  balance debits are built on the frozen shelf basis
  (SHELF_POST_COST_CENTS_PER_SECOND = 0.1, the pre-cut figure)
  and must equal the snapshots below, which were generated
  from the pricing code the day BEFORE the cut landed, for
  every per-second lane and resolution and a spread of
  durations (the debit path covers the flat-per-clip lanes
  too). If a future change wants customer prices to move,
  it must regenerate these snapshots deliberately. The same
  freeze later absorbed MAGI-2's 2026-11-26 FrameHush
  reroute: its shelf basis carries a frozen shelf provider
  rate (SHELF_PROVIDER_RATES_CENTS_PER_SECOND, the pre-
  reroute $0.08/s) over its true $0.06/s rate, so its
  snapshot figures below stand unchanged too.
- ONE deliberate exception, regenerated 2026-11-27 under
  the owner's beat-Higgsfield rule (check_debit_undercut.py
  pins the reasoning): the lanes on the debit-safe
  undercut basis, Wan 3.0 at 480p and 720p, no longer sell
  at the frozen shelf cost (3.65 / 7.2 cents per second).
  They sell at the highest rate whose whole-cent debit
  stays under Higgsfield's figure at every duration (4.0 /
  8.0), which also restores at least 25% profit over their
  true rerouted cost. Only those two cells' sell and debit
  snapshot entries moved; every minimum and every other
  lane's figures stand as generated. The same exception
  gained a third cell in the follow-up later that day:
  Wan 3.0 at 1080p sold at its hard minimum, 17.125 cents
  per second, a hair under Higgsfield's 17.208 figure,
  but the minimum is not a whole cent and its whole-cent
  debit crossed Higgsfield's total at the 2, 3, 4 and 9
  second lengths, so it now sells at the debit-safe rate
  for its tier, 17.0 cents per second (about a 75%
  margin over true cost). Only the 1080p cell's sell and
  debit snapshot entries moved with it; its minimum
  stands at 17.125 and every other lane's figures stand
  as generated.
- The finishing outputs are written faststart (moov up
  front): the five delivered-file commands in _process_file
  and _process_api_file, the two word-fix audio swaps and
  the caption burn-in all carry -movflags +faststart, so a
  newly finished file never needs the serve-time remux pass
  in ensure_web_file (proven locally 2026-11-26: the old
  finishing output is moov-last, the faststart output is
  moov-first under the app's own _moov_first test).

No Modal calls, no network, no generation.

Run:  modal-venv python scratch/check_post_cost_cut.py
"""
